==English==
{{was wotd|2006|December|4}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|arbitrarie}}, {{der|en|la|arbitrārius|t=arbitrary, uncertain}}, from {{m|la|arbiter|t=witness, on-looker, listener, judge, overseer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɑː.bɪ.tɹə.ɹi/|q2=haplology|/ˈɑː.bɪ.tɹi/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɑɹ.bɪˌtɹɛ(ə).ɹi/|a=US,Canada}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-arbitrary.ogg|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-arbitrary.ogg|a=AU}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|usually|of a decision}} Based on individual discretion or judgment; not based on any [[objective]] distinction, perhaps even made at [[random]].
#: {{ux|en|Benjamin Franklin's designation of "positive" and "negative" to different charges was '''arbitrary'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The decision to use 18 years as the legal age of adulthood was '''arbitrary''', as both age 17 and 19 were reasonable alternatives.}}
# Determined by [[impulse]] rather than [[reason]]; often connoting [[heavy-handedness]].
#* '''1937/1938''', {{w|Albert Einstein}}, [[wikiquote:Albert Einstein and politics|letter to Max Born]]
#*: {{quote|en|The Russian trials were Stalin's purges, with which he attempted to consolidate his power. Like most people in the West, I believed these show trials to be the '''arbitrary''' acts of a cruel dictator.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1906|author=w:Gelett Burgess|title=Are You a Bromide?
|passage=The bromide conforms to everything sanctioned by the majority, and may be depended upon to be trite, banal, and '''arbitrary'''.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Any, out of all that are possible.
#: {{ux|en|The equation is true for an '''arbitrary''' value of x.}}
# Determined by independent [[arbiter]].
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} Not representative or symbolic; not [[iconic]].

